blocks:
  - type: metin
    baslik: What does anxiety look like in a child?
    metin: |-
      In an adult, anxiety is mostly experienced as a thought. In a child it shows up in the body, in behaviour and in sleep. This is why an anxious child is usually described not as anxious but as grumpy, clingy, stubborn or unwell.

      A child does not say "I am worried." They say their stomach hurts, they will not go to bed, they will not leave your side, they ask the same question over and over, or they explode with an anger that seems to come from nowhere. That gap between the name of the symptom and the name of the feeling is why anxiety is one of the last things a family recognises.

      The NHS draws the line this way: anxiety becomes something to address when it is "very strong, or getting worse, and does not go away", and when it "gets in the way of daily activities, stopping your child doing things they enjoy" ([NHS, Anxiety in children](https://www.nhs.uk/mental-health/children-and-young-adults/advice-for-parents/anxiety-in-children/)).
  - type: bilgi-kutusu
    ton: yesil
    baslik: Not every anxiety is a disorder
    metin: A child afraid of the dark, a student tense before an exam, a four-year-old struggling to let go of a parent on the first day — all three are ordinary. Anxiety is a working emotion; it exists to see danger coming. **The measure is not intensity but restriction:** the picture has changed once a child starts giving up things they could otherwise do.
  - type: kart-grid
    baslik: Where the signs gather
    giris: 'Childhood anxiety needs to be read across three areas at once. One sign in one area does not carry meaning:'
    kolon: 3
    kartlar:
      - baslik: In the body
        maddeler:
          - Stomach aches and headaches
          - Nausea, changes in appetite
          - Trouble falling asleep
          - Frequent trips to the toilet
          - Nail biting, muscle tension
      - baslik: In behaviour
        maddeler:
          - Not leaving a parent's side
          - Avoiding anything new
          - Repeated questions, seeking reassurance
          - Sudden anger and tears
          - Not wanting to go to school
      - baslik: In thinking
        maddeler:
          - "What if something happens"
          - Fear of making a mistake
          - Perfectionism
          - Expecting bad news
          - Seeing themselves as not good enough
  - type: kart-yigini
    baslik: The fears each age brings
    giris: 'Every age has its own fears. A fear that fits the age also passes within a time that fits the age:'
    kartlar:
      - baslik: 8 months – 2 years
        metin: Stranger anxiety and separation anxiety. Crying when a caregiver leaves is expected here; it is a sign that attachment has formed. Where a separation is short and predictable, it settles quickly.
      - baslik: 3–5 years
        metin: The dark, sleeping alone, monsters, loud noises, animals. Imagination expands fast, and so fears take concrete shapes. Separation anxiety can reappear at the start of nursery.
      - baslik: 6–9 years
        metin: A shift towards real dangers — illness, accidents, something happening to a parent, burglars. School adds performance worries and "what if my friend doesn't like me".
      - baslik: 10–13 years
        metin: Social anxiety comes to the front. Embarrassment, exclusion, speaking in front of the class and worries about appearance are common. Exam periods are a clear trigger; the article on [coping with exam anxiety](/en/blog/coping-with-exam-anxiety.html) goes into that.
      - baslik: Adolescence
        metin: The future, identity, friendships, and the pull between separating and belonging. At this age anxiety usually appears as irritability, withdrawal or disrupted sleep, and is rarely described using the word anxiety at all.
  - type: metin
    baslik: When is separation anxiety expected, and when is it too much?
    metin: |-
      Separation anxiety appears in infancy and is an ordinary part of development. It begins at around eight months, becomes visible again around nursery and school transitions, and usually settles by itself.

      What changes the picture is how long it lasts and how far it spreads. If your child starts dreading a separation days in advance, cannot stay alone in a room at home, insists on a parent being there to fall asleep, describes recurring fears that something will happen to you, and this continues for weeks, it is no longer a phase to wait out.

      The picture it is most often confused with is school refusal. Your child may not be avoiding school but the separation itself — the article on [school refusal](/en/blog/school-refusal-in-children.html) covers the difference.
  - type: bilgi-kutusu
    ton: turuncu
    baslik: Why reassurance stops working
    metin: 'An anxious child asks, a parent answers, the child settles — and shortly afterwards asks again. This loop is where families get most worn out. The reason is this: **each reassurance lowers the anxiety in the short term, and in doing so teaches it to repeat.** What helps is not cutting reassurance off but slowly making room for your child to find their own answer — "what do you think would happen?" does more work than a direct reply.'
  - type: numarali-liste
    baslik: How to work with an anxious child at home
    giris: 'The aim is not to remove the anxiety but to let your child move while carrying it:'
    maddeler:
      - Accept the feeling first. "There is nothing to be scared of" closes the conversation; "I can see this is hard for you" keeps it open.
      - Notice the avoidance. Avoidance is where anxiety grows. Spend a week noting which situations your child steers around.
      - Make the steps smaller. Between not going to the party at all and staying two hours there are many rungs. Choose the rung together.
      - Make separations predictable. Say goodbye briefly, clearly and the same way each time. Slipping out unseen does not reduce anxiety; it makes the next separation harder.
      - Keep your own anxiety separate. Children read a parent's tension. Talking your worry through somewhere other than in front of your child is part of the work.
    kapanis: These steps take weeks to show. Working with anxiety moves less through the anxiety easing and more through a child seeing that they can take a step while it is still there.
  - type: bilgi-kutusu
    ton: turuncu
    baslik: When to talk to a professional
    metin: |-
      Where anxiety has run for weeks without easing · where your child has started giving up on school, sleep or friendships · where physical complaints have been checked by a doctor and no cause has been found · where sleep has come apart · where there are sudden, intense episodes that look like panic · or where family life has begun to organise itself around your child's anxiety.

      None of these settles a diagnosis in advance. They only mean that waiting for the anxiety to pass on its own has stopped working.
  - type: onayli-liste
    baslik: A few things specific to North Cyprus
    giris: 'Families on the island meet this picture alongside a few extra conditions:'
    maddeler:
      - baslik: Changing school, changing language
        aciklama: Anxiety is common in a child moving into a different education system or a different language. The signs usually cluster in the first months of the transition and settle as the child does — and are looked at when they do not.
      - baslik: Growing up apart from a parent
        aciklama: Where one parent works off the island, separation anxiety can run longer. Contact that is regular and predictable helps more than contact that is long.
      - baslik: Being visible in a small community
        aciklama: The sense that everyone knows everyone can raise both a child's social anxiety and a family's hesitation about seeking support. At our centre, sessions and your personal information are protected in line with our confidentiality principles.
      - baslik: Working in two languages
        aciklama: Sessions can be held in whichever language your child explains themselves in more comfortably. Having English alongside Turkish makes starting easier for international families.
  - type: bilgi-kutusu
    ton: yesil
    baslik: Where to start in Famagusta
    metin: Work with childhood anxiety usually runs along two lines — sessions with the child, and the parent taking part in the process. Our [child psychology](/en/services/child-psychology.html) and [adolescent psychology](/en/services/adolescent-psychology.html) services cover that work; where the anxiety is bound up with a transition inside the family, [family therapy](/en/services/family-therapy.html) is discussed separately. If you are wondering how anxiety looks in adults, the article on [anxiety disorders](/en/blog/what-is-anxiety-disorder.html) covers it. If you would like to talk, we are here.
